{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "Cooperstown's second passenger railroad, and the one that actually ran into the village streets: the electric interurban promoted in 1901 as the 'Leatherstocking Route.' It began in 1888 as the horse-drawn Oneonta Street Railway, became the Oneonta & Otego Valley Railroad in 1897, and as the Oneonta, Cooperstown & Richfield Springs Railway pushed north from Oneonta - reaching Laurens in July 1901, COOPERSTOWN in September 1901, Richfield Springs in the summer of 1902 and Mohawk (opposite Herkimer, on the NYC and the Erie Canal) by 1906. Later names: Oneonta & Mohawk Valley Railway (1906-08), Otsego & Herkimer Railroad (1908-16), Southern New York Power & Railway (1916-24), Southern New York Railway (1924-1970). Standard gauge, single track, overhead electric; the company also sold electricity along the line until 1924. At its peak it worked about 65 miles of track with 109 train movements a day. Cooperstown sat at the end of a roughly 4.5 km branch running north-east from a junction near Index/Phoenix Mills; the through line to Richfield Springs carried on north-west through Toddsville, Fly Creek and Oaksville. Ridership collapsed from 61,403 riders in 1928 to 14,118 in 1931, and the SNY ended rail passenger service in 1933 in favour of the buses it had started running in 1926. Full stop order Oneonta-Mohawk: Oneonta - West Oneonta - Laurens - Mount Vision - South Hartwick - Hartwick - Index - (branch to Cooperstown) - Toddsville - Fly Creek - Oaksville - Schuyler Lake - Canadarago Park - Lake House - Richfield Springs - Jordanville - Henderson - McCredys - Mohawk.", "sources": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_New_York_Railway (45.5 miles Mohawk-Oneonta in Otsego and Herkimer counties; Oneonta Street Railway 1888; reached Laurens July 1901, Cooperstown September 1901, Richfield Springs summer 1902, Mohawk 1906; standard gauge, single track, overhead electric; ridership 61,403 in 1928 to 14,118 in 1931; rail passenger service ended 1933; Cooperstown branch closed 1940; freight ended May 7, 1970 and track lifted July 1971; full stop list)", "https://www.vizettes.com/kt/ne-interurbans/ny/4-uo.htm (company name succession 1900-1926; 65 miles of track and 109 trains daily at peak)", "https://www.american-rails.com/nywintstrecrs.html", "https://penneyandkc.wordpress.com/the-southern-new-york-railway/"], "geometry_sources": ["OpenStreetMap (via Overpass API): ways named 'Southern New York Railway' tagged railway=abandoned, routed by Dijkstra shortest path. The alignment survives as abandoned grade for most of its length but is fragmentary. Oneonta-Cooperstown is 41.4 km traced, of which 4.6 km (11%) is straight-line bridging across gaps where no grade is mapped. Cooperstown-Mohawk is 53.3 km traced, of which 8.3 km (16%) is straight-line bridging - the largest single gaps are around Canadarago Park/Richfield Springs and between Oaksville and Schuyler Lake. Both approximations follow the correctly-ordered real stop towns and are flagged here rather than presented as surveyed geometry.", "The southern end of the mapped grade is about 2.3 km west of downtown Oneonta; the street trackage the interurban used through Oneonta itself is not mapped in OSM and is NOT extrapolated here, so the line stops short of Oneonta's Main Street."], "not_yet_sourced": "Exact timetables. The 109-daily-trains figure is a system-wide peak count, not a headway for any one segment, so the frequencies below are estimates. The Oneonta city streetcar network (which outlived the interurban cars until 1926) is not traced.", "pulled": "20260824", "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6.", "interurban_audit": "2026-08-24: features on this layer that are inter-city electric railways were retagged mode='interurban' (previously 'streetcar' or 'intercity_rail') so the map can draw them distinctly from city streetcars and from steam roads. See CLAUDE.md Rule 7."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Southern New York Railway (Oneonta, Cooperstown & Richfield Springs Ry.)", "line": "Southern New York: Oneonta \u2013 Cooperstown", "id": "sny-oneonta-cooperstown", "color": "#c1440e", "mode": "interurban", "avg_speed_kmh": 26, "speed_source": "estimated: single-track overhead-electric interurban on largely private right of way with street running at each end; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1901, "end": 1905, "headway_min": 90, "note": "Opened north from Oneonta as the Oneonta, Cooperstown & Richfield Springs Railway, reaching Laurens in July 1901 and Cooperstown in September 1901 - about 25 miles, promoted as the 'Leatherstocking Route' after James Fenimore Cooper. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_New_York_Railway", "https://www.vizettes.com/kt/ne-interurbans/ny/4-uo.htm", "https://www.american-rails.com/nywintstrecrs.html", "https://penneyandkc.wordpress.com/the-southern-new-york-railway/"]}, {"start": 1906, "end": 1927, "headway_min": 75, "note": "Peak years, under the Oneonta & Mohawk Valley Railway (1906-08), Otsego & Herkimer Railroad (1908-16) and Southern New York Power & Railway (1916-24). The system worked about 65 miles of track with roughly 109 train movements a day, and sold electric power along the line until 1924. Cooperstown was reached by a branch from the junction near Index. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_New_York_Railway", "https://www.vizettes.com/kt/ne-interurbans/ny/4-uo.htm", "https://www.american-rails.com/nywintstrecrs.html", "https://penneyandkc.wordpress.com/the-southern-new-york-railway/"]}, {"start": 1928, "end": 1933, "headway_min": 180, "note": "Steep decline: 61,403 riders in 1928 fell to 14,118 by 1931 as the company's own buses, started in 1926, took the traffic. The Southern New York ended rail passenger service in 1933; the Cooperstown branch itself closed in 1940. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_New_York_Railway", "https://www.vizettes.com/kt/ne-interurbans/ny/4-uo.htm", "https://www.american-rails.com/nywintstrecrs.html", "https://penneyandkc.wordpress.com/the-southern-new-york-railway/"]}]}, "geometry": {"type": "LineString", "coordinates": [[-75.09197, 42.44598], [-75.09223, 42.44608], [-75.09269, 42.44609], [-75.09305, 42.44606], [-75.0937, 42.44606], [-75.09386, 42.44652], [-75.09398, 42.4469], [-75.09406, 42.44707], [-75.09421, 42.44724], [-75.09559, 42.4475], [-75.09809, 42.4486], [-75.09946, 42.44983], [-75.10005, 42.45036], [-75.10063, 42.45097], [-75.10102, 42.45154], [-75.10122, 42.45208], [-75.10117, 42.45268], [-75.10089, 42.45397], [-75.10052, 42.45499], [-75.10057, 42.45511], [-75.10066, 42.45538], [-75.10083, 42.4556], [-75.1012