erurban", "avg_speed_kmh": 26, "speed_source": "estimated: single-track overhead-electric interurban on largely private right of way; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1902, "end": 1905, "headway_min": 120, "note": "Extended north from the Cooperstown area through Toddsville, Fly Creek, Oaksville and Schuyler Lake to the resort town of Richfield Springs in the summer of 1902. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_New_York_Railway", "https://www.vizettes.com/kt/ne-interurbans/ny/4-uo.htm", "https://www.american-rails.com/nywintstrecrs.html", "https://penneyandkc.wordpress.com/the-southern-new-york-railway/"]}, {"start": 1906, "end": 1927, "headway_min": 90, "note": "Completed through Jordanville to Mohawk in 1906, giving Cooperstown an electric connection to the New York Central main line and the Erie Canal at Mohawk/Herkimer - 45.5 miles end to end. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_New_York_Railway", "https://www.vizettes.com/kt/ne-interurbans/ny/4-uo.htm", "https://www.american-rails.com/nywintstrecrs.html", "https://penneyandkc.wordpress.com/the-southern-new-york-railway/"]}, {"start": 1928, "end": 1933, "headway_min": 240, "note": "Passenger traffic collapsed and the Southern New York ended rail passenger service in 1933 to concentrate on freight and its bus operations. Freight lingered until May 7, 1970 and the track was lifted in July 1971. Frequency estimated, unsou